A||||4||Wannabe||Blowing Oil||||1||
Z||000000||Wannabe||08-13-2001||08:55 PM||||During racing last weekend, I sometimes notice some puffs of smoke coming up from then engine vents. Some observers at trackside reported that the car would smoke under braking and in thinking back, it seemes to be combined braking and downshifting, when the engine is being revved very high. After a weekend of racing, there is now a buildup of oil down the front side of the oil pan and out back onto the rubber skirts around the cradle. I also noticed that the dip stick keeps popping out and the problem became worse after it was tie wrapped to keep it in place. It seems as though the crankcase is being pressurized and blowing oil out from the valve cover seal onto the front exhaust manifold. Why might this be happening? The car only has about 50K miles, so the bottom end should be in decent shape. I plan to do a compresion check to be sure though. Any comments or experience with this sort of thing?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Primaris||08-13-2001||09:39 PM||||This is second hand info but check your PVC valve.  I asked a mech why I was loosing oil out of my oil cap and he told me to replace the PVC valve.  Let me know the results.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wannabe||08-13-2001||11:10 PM||||PCV valve is new.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||08-13-2001||11:43 PM||||Assuming this is a V6 since you said front manifold. Check the hose on the front valve cover (about the same size as the PCV hose). It originally ran to a steel tube along the firewall to the air cleaner. The recall done on most cars rerouted it to run to the big air cleaner to throttle body tube. <P>If that tube and the PCV are both in working order, you shouldn't get pressure buildup like that in the crankcase unless you have one or more bad rings. Do a compression check next.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||08-16-2001||10:06 AM||||I found the source of the leak, but have not had time to do a compression check. All the hoses check out (uh oh) . As it stands, I found that the oil pan has at least one pinhole that you can see. This is where the oil is coming from. It really is bizarre that the car is amost completely rust free, and yet the oil pan has rusted to the point of having holes in it. With a Northstar swap on the horizon, it hardly seems worthwhile to replace the pan.||||reg||1
